from ./Config import Config
from ./Fonts import Fonts
from ./Output import Output
from ./OutputSize import OutputSize
from ./componentProperties/html/HtmlTag import HtmlTag

export anchor HtmlOutput:
    description:
        How a composition is turned into HTML and CSS. The language carries
        little information beyond layout and look, so the output is kept
        plain. Nearly everything is a `div`. The HTML is expected to look the
        same as the SVG and PNG, as described in @{Output}.

    files:
        Each rendered composition becomes one HTML file in the output
        directory set in @{Config}, named after the composition, such as
        `MyComposition.html`. Its CSS is in a single style element in its
        head, and the only thing it loads from elsewhere is the Google Fonts
        stylesheet.

    elements:
        Each instance becomes one element. The element is a `div` unless the
        instance's component sets @{HtmlTag}. Children become child elements,
        in the order they are written.

    root:
        The composition's root is the body element. It has no styling beyond
        the reset described below, and the canvas size and background. The
        body is given the canvas width from @{OutputSize}, and its height
        unless that is `auto`, and clips anything outside it.

    text:
        Each quoted text in a body becomes a text node, in the position it is
        written among the children. Characters with a meaning in HTML, such as
        `<` and `&`, are escaped, so text is always shown as written and never
        read as markup.

    attributes:
        Each attribute on an instance becomes an HTML attribute of the same
        name and value on its element. A path in a `src` attribute is resolved
        as described for images in values, and the file is copied into the
        output directory alongside the HTML.

    classes:
        Each component used in the composition becomes one CSS class, named
        after the name the component was declared with, not any alias it was
        imported under. If two different components were declared with the
        same name, in different files, the later ones are given a numeric
        suffix, such as `Button-2`. An element gets exactly one class, the class of
        its own component.

        Two markers carry layout details that CSS classes can't express, and
        never carry styling of their own:

        - flow
            A block whose children are all blocks gets the attribute
            `data-emul-flow` set to `column`, which lays it out as a flex column
            so margins never collapse, as described in the layout rules.
        - clamp
            The text of an element with lineClamp is wrapped in a span with the
            attribute `data-emul-clamp`, which carries the clamp. Browsers only
            hide clamped lines when the clamped box is no taller than its text,
            and the element itself may be stretched taller by its parent.

        Inheritance is resolved before the CSS is written. Each class holds
        the component's complete set of properties, after merging everything
        it extends and applying the shorthand rule. Emul's inheritance never
        relies on CSS combining several classes.

    properties:
        Each property becomes the CSS property it corresponds to, with the
        same value. Shorthands are written out as the specific properties
        they set, so the shorthand rule gives the same result in CSS as in
        Emul.

        There are two exceptions:

        - htmlTag
            Sets the element's tag name, and is not written as CSS at all.
        - lineClamp
            Is written as the CSS browsers need for it to work today, which is
            `-webkit-line-clamp` together with the display, box orientation
            and overflow settings it depends on.

    fonts:
        The head links the Google Fonts stylesheet for exactly the families,
        weights and styles the composition uses, as described in @{Fonts}.

    reset:
        The stylesheet begins with a reset rule that applies to every
        element. It removes all browser default styling, then sets every
        property to its Emul initial value. Because every element sets every
        inheritable CSS property explicitly, nothing cascades from parent to
        child. An element looks the way its own component says, and no other
        way.

        The reset also sets `box-sizing` to `border-box`, matching Emul's
        sizing, where width and height include padding and border.

    example:
        ```emul
        \\\
        component Card:
            padding: 24px
            backgroundColor: #ffffff

        component Title:
            htmlTag: h2
            fontSize: 20px

        composition Example {
            Card {
                Title { "Hello" }
                "Plain text inside the card"
            }
        }
        \\\
        ```

        This becomes a body holding a `div` with the class `Card`. Inside it
        are an `h2` with the class `Title` holding the text "Hello", then the
        text node "Plain text inside the card".
